#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Settings > System > Storage > Free up space: measures what can be
cleared, and clears it.

Half of it is the person's own files and half is the system's, so there
are two halves:

  As the person, never as root (nothing in a home folder is ever touched
  with root's rights):
    measure-home              the Recycle Bins on internal drives, app
                              caches, leftovers of removed apps in the
                              home folder, large files in Downloads
    run ITEM... [--download PATH]...
                              clear the ticked items; the system ones go
                              through pkexec (clean), then the home ones
    run --auto                Tidy up automatically: only when less than 10%
                              of the disk is free, only the items ticked
                              when it was turned on, never Downloads; says
                              what it removed in a notification

  As root, through pkexec, each request under its own polkit action:
    measure                   (no password) old restore points, update
                              files, leftovers of removed packages,
                              Windows mode's disk
    measure open-files        (no password) which files under the asking
                              person's cache folders their programs have
                              open, so App caches leaves them
    clean ITEM...             (admin password) clear those of them
    auto-system               (no password) the same for Tidy up
                              automatically: does nothing unless less than
                              10% of the disk is free, and clears only what
                              set-auto stored for the person asking
    set-auto [ITEM...]        (admin password) store which system items
                              Tidy up automatically may clear for the
                              person asking; none turns it off

ITEMs are the page's: bin, restorePoints, updates, caches, leftovers,
winmode, downloads. Clearing never takes a list of paths from the caller
(Review's downloads are only checked against the list measured here): it
measures again and removes exactly what measuring would show.

Sizes are what the disk would really get back, not file lengths: blocks
in use, each hard-linked file counted once, and on Btrfs only what no
other snapshot shares. What was cleared is noted in the journal as
"FREED {json}", which Settings > System > History shows.
"""

